.-
help for ^dentrace^                                           [STB-16: snp6]
.-

Density trace (boxcar weight function) updated               
----------------------------------------------

         ^dentrace^ varname [^if^ exp] [^in^ range],
                 ^h^val^(^#^)^ ^f^code^(^#^)^ [^np^oints^(^#^)^ 
                 ^g^en^(^denvar^ ^midvar^)^ ^nog^raph graph_options]

Description
-----------
This program calculates the density trace of "varname" using two weight
functions (boxcar and cosine) as described in Chambers et. al. (1983)
and graph the results.  


Options
-------

^h^val^(^#^)^ is a constant that specifies the width of the density window 
around each point.  

^f^code^(^#^)^ is the code for the weigth function: 1 boxcar; 2 cosine

^np^oints^(^#^)^ permits to especify the number of points to be used for 
estimation

^g^en permits to generate two new variables: "denvar" with the density 
values and "midval" with the variable that contains the points used for
calculation.

^nog^raph suppresses the graph drawing.

graph_options are any of the options allowed with ^graph, twoway^.

Remarks
-------

^h^val and ^f^code are not optional. If the user does not provide
them, the program halts and displays an error message on screen.

This procedure can be regarded as a descriptive smoother of histograms.
Indeed this simple density estimator is like a histogram using bins of the
specified width but no fixed origin; the bin's windows run along the 
series with each observation at its midpoint allowing overlap.
The "smoothness" of the result can be regulated by changing the width of
the interval. Wide intervals produce smooth results; narrow intervals
result in noiser density values.

WARNING: Even though ^dentrace^ considers by default only 50 equally 
spaced bins, the computing time required is proportional to the number 
of observations. Please be patient.

Example
-------

  . ^dentrace ozone, h(75) f(1) gen(dtrace midpt)^

After some time (depending on the number of observations) the results are
displayed as a density graph and two variables with the results "dtrace"
and "midpoint" are generated. 

It is possible to draw the graphic with the generated results by typing:

  . ^graph dtrace midpt^, xlab ylab c(l) s(p)


References
----------

   Chambers, J.M., W.S. Cleveland, B. Kleiner and P.A. Tukey (1983)
        Graphical Methods for Data Analysis. Wadsworth & Brooks/Cole
        Chap. 2: 9-46.
   Fox, J. (1990) Describing univariate distributions. In (Fox, J. &
        J.S. Long, Eds.) "Modern Methods of Data Analysis". Sage
        Chap. 2: 58-125.


